# ── Build output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The build chain (src/makefile.py) writes everything it produces under
# _temp/ at this repository's root: the compiler output, the downloaded Lua /
# Python / Raylib / FreeFont trees, and the generated embed sources. None of it
# is source, and _temp/ is large. Keeping it out of the tree is what stops a
# build from dirtying the working directory and blocking `git pull`.
_temp/

# A project built with `gcl -build` drops the executable and its bundle in the
# project's out/ directory; the bundle is a pack of the runtime and the
# scripts, not something to commit.
*.gcBundle
out/

# ── Compiler output ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The makefile keeps its objects under _temp/, but a manual gcc or a tool that
# does not follow that rule can still leave one behind. There is no blanket
# `*.so`/`*.dll` here for a reason: the built Lua and Python runtimes carry
# those extensions and live under _temp/ (ignored above), so the negation is
# not needed - but a stray object is caught.
*.o
*.a
*.exe
*.out
*.obj
# NOT a blanket `*.obj`: the Wavefront models under examples/ (terrain.obj,
# water.obj, cube.obj) share that extension and are project CONTENT. Ignoring
# them kept every model out of the repository, so a fresh clone ran the fps
# demo with no terrain, water or cube at all. The negations below keep them.
!**/assets/*.obj

# ── Python embed (downloaded, not source) ───────────────────────────────────
*.pyd
*.zip
*.cat
*.pth

# ── Python cache ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# ── OS and editor leftovers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.vscode/
.idea/
nul
